## Preliminaries: check your setup

Evaluate this cell after setup finishes. A **satisfiable** result showing `ready`
confirms that the bundled clingo is running inside the notebook. Each solve
closes its own native control; this check is safe to reevaluate.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../support/course.exs", __DIR__))
alias ASPCourse, as: Course
alias Clingo.Control

Clingo.solve!("ready. #show ready/0.") |> Course.display()
```

## Make row and column constraints explicit

### What you will learn

Build a complete small Latin square, then add a clue.

### Fill a three-by-three Latin square

Each cell chooses one value. Pairwise constraints prevent duplicate values in each row and column. Every value ranges over 1..3. There are twelve Latin squares of this size before clues are imposed.

```elixir
encoding = ~S"""
index(1..3).
1 { cell(Row,Col,Value) : index(Value) } 1 :- index(Row), index(Col).
:- cell(Row,C1,Value), cell(Row,C2,Value), C1 < C2.
:- cell(R1,Col,Value), cell(R2,Col,Value), R1 < R2.
#show cell/3.
"""

result = Clingo.solve!(encoding, models: :all)
true = result.exhausted
12 = length(result.models)
true = Enum.all?(result.models, &(length(&1.shown) == 9))
Course.display(result)
```

### Constrain a clue rather than overwrite a cell

A clue rejects assignments whose upper-left value is not 1. Four squares remain. Sudoku adds block constraints; other grid puzzles change the local constraints, not the need for explicit domains.

```elixir
clued = Clingo.solve!(encoding <> " :- not cell(1,1,1).", models: :all)
4 = length(clued.models)
true = Enum.all?(clued.models, &("cell(1,1,1)" in &1.shown))
Course.display(clued)
```

### Try it yourself

Add a second clue requiring cell(1,2,1). to the already clued Latin square. Predict the status.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The first row would repeat value 1, so no model remains. An integrity constraint such as :- not cell(1,2,1). expresses the clue without introducing a new head.

</details>

## Fill a Latin square

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/modeling", __DIR__))
```

Fill an n-by-n board with values 1 through n. No value may repeat within a row or within a column. The input facts define the instance; the next editable cell is the encoding you must complete. Show solutions through `latin/3`.

The starter is intentionally incomplete. A satisfiable run of that starter does not establish that it solves the exercise. The reference encoding later in the notebook provides a comparison once you have written your own rules.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "instances/instance-latin.lp", ~S"""
size(3).
""")
```

### Complete the rules

Keep the input format and `latin/3` output contract. Start with a rule that generates candidates, then exclude candidates that break the conditions above.

> **Note.** Terminate #show latin/3 with a period. The exercise encoding remains blank.

```elixir
~S"""
#show latin/3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-latin.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

The solve combines the instance file with your inline rules. `files:` supplies the instance; the string supplies the encoding.

### Compare with the reference

Alternative encoding: [latin.lp](../assets/modeling/solutions/latin.lp). [Test input](../assets/modeling/instances/instance-latin.lp).

Compare your encoding with this readable reference. The collection files provide another formulation; compare projected model sets rather than enumeration order.

```elixir
~S"""
index(1..N) :- size(N).
1 { latin(R,C,V) : index(V) } 1 :- index(R), index(C).
:- latin(R,C,V), latin(R,D,V), C < D.
:- latin(R,C,V), latin(S,C,V), R < S.
#show latin/3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-latin.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Workspace cleanup

Save edited files before running cleanup. Rerun this section’s workspace cell to start again.

```elixir
File.rm_rf!(workdir)
:ok
```

## Complete a Sudoku board

Exercise files: [sudoku.lp](../assets/sudoku/sudoku.lp).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/sudoku", __DIR__))
```

Fill the 9-by-9 board with digits 1 through 9. Preserve every given digit. Each row, column and 3-by-3 box must contain each digit exactly once.

Complete the starter encoding, then verify the givens and every row, column, box or region constraint on the resulting models.

### Keep the data contract

The givens use `initial/3`. Keep the template’s solution predicate and show directive. Generate a value for each cell, then enforce uniqueness in the three kinds of unit.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "instances/ex00.lp", ~S"""
initial(1,1,5). initial(1,2,3). initial(1,5,7).
initial(2,1,6). initial(2,4,1). initial(2,5,9). initial(2,6,5).
initial(3,2,9). initial(3,3,8). initial(3,8,6).
initial(4,1,8). initial(4,5,6). initial(4,9,3).
initial(5,1,4). initial(5,4,8). initial(5,6,3). initial(5,9,1).
initial(6,1,7). initial(6,5,2). initial(6,9,6).
initial(7,2,6). initial(7,7,2). initial(7,8,8).
initial(8,4,4). initial(8,5,1). initial(8,6,9). initial(8,9,5).
initial(9,5,8). initial(9,8,7). initial(9,9,9).
""")
```

### Develop and check the encoding

Edit the copied template and test it with the input above. Check each required constraint and download your edits before cleanup.

```elixir
Course.archive(workdir, "sudoku.zip")
```

```elixir
~S"""
subgrid_size(3).
#show sudoku/3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/ex00.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Check boundary cases

Test the smallest instance first, then check each derived relation. Compare projected model sets and, when optimizing, proven costs.

### Workspace cleanup

Save edited files before running cleanup. Rerun this section’s workspace cell to start again.

```elixir
File.rm_rf!(workdir)
:ok
```

## Illuminate a grid

Exercise files: [ex01.lp](../assets/light/instances/ex01.lp) · [light.lp](../assets/light/light.lp) · [ex01.json](../assets/light/solutions/ex01.json).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/light", __DIR__))
```

Place lamps in ordinary cells. Every ordinary cell must be illuminated horizontally or vertically, and blocked cells stop light rays. Two lamps must not see each other. A numbered blocked cell requires exactly that many orthogonally adjacent lamps.

Complete the starter encoding before comparing its projected models with the supplied solution data.

### Keep the data contract

The board uses `rows/1`, `cols/1`, `digit/3` and `empty/2`. Show lamp coordinates with `light/2`. Check the ray relation before adding the numbered-cell constraints.

### Develop and check the encoding

Complete the editable encoding below. Compare its shown atom sets with the JSON answers in `solutions`; `optimal_cost` records a proven optimum when present. Download your edits before cleanup.

```elixir
Course.archive(workdir, "light.zip")
```

```elixir
~S"""
#show light/2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/ex01.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Check boundary cases

Test the smallest instance first, then check each derived relation. Compare projected model sets and, when optimizing, proven costs.

