## Move numbered pieces into regions

Exercise files: [instance01.lp](../assets/yosenabe/instances/instance01.lp) · [yosenabe.lp](../assets/yosenabe/yosenabe.lp).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/yosenabe", __DIR__))
```

Send each numbered piece along a straight grid route into a region. Routes must not touch or cross, every region needs a piece, and a region with a target requires exactly that sum of incoming values. A route may pass through another region; its destination is the first cell of the region where it stops.

Complete the starter encoding, then verify the givens and every row, column, box or region constraint on the resulting models.

### Keep the data contract

Inputs are `cell/2`, `area/3`, `number/3` and optional `goal/2`. Show each source/destination pair through `target/4`. Distinguish the cells traversed by a route from the region that receives its value.

### Complete the model

Implement the conditions in the problem description without hard-coding an instance. Keep the required show signature so results can be compared with the reference data.

### Develop and check the encoding

Edit the copied template and test it with the input above. Check each required constraint and download your edits before cleanup.

```elixir
Course.archive(workdir, "yosenabe.zip")
```

```elixir
~S"""
dir(0,1). dir(0,-1). dir(1,0). dir(-1,0).
#show target/4.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance01.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Check boundary cases

Test the smallest instance first, then check each derived relation. Compare projected model sets and, when optimizing, proven costs.

